a fiery prophecy zombie pilgrims and a  poisoned poisoner Wednesday ended with  such a bang that you may have missed how  the pieces fit together  everything happening in the explosive  finale of Wednesday is tied to a  maniacal colonizer who founded the  village of Jericho named Joseph  crackstone he's a vile and devious man  who hates outcasts even though the  outcasts lived in this part of North  America before he and his ilk ever  arrived there he wants to eradicate them  from the face of the Earth his Prejudice  sets the stage for the show's  exploration of bigotry through a  Fantastical lens one of those outcasts  is an ancestor of Wednesday Adams who  also happens to look exactly like her  named goodie Adams her family is killed  by crack Stone but she escapes in Val's  Vengeance she kills crack Stone curses  his soul and puts him in a casket sealed  with a blood lock to keep him from ever  returning  when Wednesday first visits Jericho  Sheriff Donovan tells her exactly what  he thinks of her father the man belongs  Behind Bars from murder  upon further investigation Wednesday  discovers that when her parents were  students at Nevermore Gomez was accused  of killing an army named Garrett Gates  the truth is that Garrett was a  descendant of Joseph crackstone tasked  with killing everyone at Nevermore with  Nightshade poisoning however Garrett was  so obsessed with Morticia that he tried  to kill Gomez so that he could have her  during their altercation of Isle of  nightshade poison shattered in his  pocket killing him  following the death of Garrett his  sister Laurel flees the country and is  believed to have drowned later on  however Laurel Gates fakes her death  like the rest of her family she hates  outcasts and can't stand the fact that  they built a school for them on her  ancestors land and killed her brother so  Laurel builds a new identity Marilyn  Thornhill she returns to Jericho and  manages to secure a teaching position at  Nevermore the school sees having a  normie on the staff as a first step in  bringing outcasts and normies together  but it's all a ruse thornhill's Mission  from the very beginning is to resurrect  her Fallen ancestor and allow him to  finish his work this line of thinking is  reminiscent to Modern racists who preach  the virtues of the good old days but  Thornhill takes it to the extreme by  literally bringing someone from the old  days back to achieve his bigoted goals  typically I have great admiration for  well-executed Revenge plots but yours  was a bit extreme  the ending of the season was prophesized  around the same time Morticia and Gomez  attended Nevermore a fellow student who  is a powerful Seer has a vision of the  school Bernie she recreates the image in  a book that is locked away in a library  hidden in the lower regions of Nevermore  it depicts a pilgrim battling a  pigtailed young girl a student is so  convinced that the girl in the picture  will destroy the school that she  eventually orders her son Rowan to kill  her if he sees her as it turns out  though psychic Visions aren't the most  reliable when it comes to seeing the  future we do see Wednesday confronting a  pilgrim but she isn't there to destroy  the school she's saving it this again  plays into the idea that the way we see  the world can be obscured by our biases  as Morticia informs Wednesday psychic  Visions are personalized if you're  optimistic or a dove you see happy  Visions if you're pessimistic or a raven  you see Doom and Gloom the woman who  prophesies the final battle must have  been a raven because she assumed  disaster was coming when it was actually  salvation  Nevermore was created to be a safe haven  where outcasts can learn and socialize  without the fear of being discriminated  against of course if you put enough  people together clicks and social groups  start to form such as the case when  Wednesday arrives while everyone there  is considered to be strange and  different they still more or less  interact the way normies do Wednesday is  too weird even for them that doesn't  mean she's incapable of making friends  however she eventually finds a group of  her own her roommate Enid is a werewolf  who can't transform Eugene spends all  his time with bees and has zero friends  Xavier grew up rich with a famous father  but he's nothing like him even Bianca  the coolest of the cool kids doesn't  quite fit in because she's a siren her  voice can control people so she never  knows that the people around her  genuinely like her or are compelled by  her boys to do what she says this  unifies them and helps create the  perfect team to stand up the Thornhill  and crack Stone  we eventually learned that goody Adams  founded a secret society known as the  nightshades in the early days of  Nevermore to protect outcasts from  vindictive normies Wednesday discovers  the location of their headquarters by  matching the watermark on the drawing of  the prophecy when the statue of Edgar  Allan Poe and solving riddle as it turns  out the society still exists and they  invite Wednesday to join them she  declines because even a secret society  is too social for her still the fact  that she was able to discover them earns  her some points they don't all become  her friends exactly but when she informs  them of the fight that is about to take  place they stand with her to protect  Nevermore and outcasts in general  when Wednesday arrives at Nevermore  there's a monster killing people the  sheriff has been telling everyone it's a  bear but Bears don't remove body parts  and leave with them tell the bear attack  you don't really believe that share no I  don't  through her investigation Wednesday  discovers the monster is called a hide  these beasts are human but transform  when triggered by dramatic events or  chemical injections the identity of the  Hide is the main mystery carrying the  series hides are tied to the people who  liberate them considering them to be  their masters Thornhill knows this and  she also knows that the sheriff's son  Tyler is a hide following the death of  his mother Thornhill manipulates him and  becomes a surrogate parental figure she  then chains him to a cave injects him  with her Elixir and forces him to  transform into a hide to kill people and  steal their body parts to help her  resurrect crackstone  like many of the characters in the show  Wednesday's perky and colorful roommate  Enid is at war with herself on the one  hand she seems to get along with most of  the kids at school and always has a  sunny disposition but she's incapable of  wolfing out and is ashamed of that for  most of the show The mosa changes is her  nails she's worried that her inability  to transform will lead to her family  disowning her if she isn't part of a  pack werewolf's Society will shun her  and she will die alone this fear drives  most of her decisions however things  start to change when she stands up to  her mother and proclaims she is her own  person from that point on she stops  operating out of fear and begins acting  out of love during the blood moon when  Nevermore is under attack she finally  Wolves Out subduing Tyler in his hide  form and protecting Wednesday  [Music]  the Severus Snape in this Harry Potter  Prestige is principal Weems although she  is a major antagonist in the series she  isn't the villain viewers are led to  think she'd be upon first encountering  her in fact every decision she makes was  meant to protect Wednesday Nevermore an  outcasts in general she wants to make  sure outcasts are never seen as evil or  dangerous so they would be accepted by  normies what's so tragic about this is  the fact that her memories of Nevermore  aren't the rosiest while Morticia and  Gomez remember Nevermore being the  greatest years of their lives she  recalls mostly disappointment and  loneliness such good times we had didn't  we Larissa  some of us better than others  she has every reason to be the villain  but she sees the good in people and that  drives her to always do what she  believes is the right thing yes  Wednesday and her friends save the  school from Thornhill and crack Stone  but Weems dedicates her life to  protecting Nevermore and that makes her  a true hero  Eugene is a stereotypical nerd who is so  isolated from his peers that the mere  fact that his classmates are willing to  visit his beekeeping Sanctuary makes him  so excited he essentially pledges his  life to them it would be easy to write  him off as desperate for companionship  but it's a little more complicated than  that Eugene doesn't just help Wednesday  with her investigation because he wants  a friend he does so because he believes  in his own fuzzy moral code  snitches get stung it's Hive code  basically Eugene understands that every  bee in a hive works together to Serve  and Protect the queen he's so in tune  with these creatures that he adapts  their social structure to his own life  during the PO cup Wednesday has a vision  when touching Joseph crackstone's Crypt  in it she is visited by her ancestor  goody who tells her  you might think that this is a metaphor  but nope it's literal after all goody  trapped cracked Stone's body in a casket  sealed with a blood lock as Thornhill  explains in the final episode the only  way to open it is to Spill the blood of  an atoms on the night of a Blood Moon  Thornhill sacrifices Wednesday and  successfully resurrects crackstone  however that's not the only key goodie  was talking about Wednesday is also the  only person who can Vanquish crack stone  for Good by stabbing him in his heart as  she lies there dying the spirit of goody  passes through her thanks to her  necklace and heals her wounds freeing  her to face crack Stone and end his  reign of terror forever  the town of Jericho serves as a visual  metaphor for the division happening in  the series it's a quaint Town built on  Stolen land with a massive School meant  for outcasts looming over it although  the money Nevermore brings into the town  keeps it thriving the citizens there  hate the fact that weirdos are living on  their land they have an amusement park  called Pilgrim world that teaches people  all about the early days of the Town  settlement it is mentioned in the series  itself that this worship of pilgrims  ignores the genocide of native people  and whitewashes history Beyond paying  lip service to this however the show  examines this dichotomy through a visual  metaphor by aligning themselves with the  idealized and inaccurate depictions of  pilgrims the town of Jericho makes a  clear statement that they believe  natives don't deserve to exist on their  land the land Nevermore was built on  didn't belong to Joseph crackstone it  was reclaimed by the outcasts he tried  to annihilate the final showdown between  Wednesday and crack Stone seems like an  intentional comment on colonization as  it is literally the descendant of a long  line of outcasts Wednesday who battles  an actual colonizer crackstone  in the end we find out Tyler was the  hide learned that Thornhill was  controlling him and finally see  crackstone destroyed also Wednesday  learns the importance of hugging she  even accepts a dreaded cell phone to  stay in touch with everyone it seems  like everything is wrapped up nice and  neat until the final scene as paramedics  transport Tyler to the hospital he  begins transforming into hide form  suggesting that we haven't seen the end  of him additionally there is still the  question of Wednesday's stalker when  examining a music box found in Laurel  Gates's room she finds a secret  compartment with photos of herself  clearly someone has been following her  and taking snapshots the easy assumption  is that it was Miss Thornhill but if  that's the case why does Wednesday get  text messages from an unknown number on  her new phone from a number she doesn't  recognize beyond that why do those  messages contain new photos of Wednesday  presumably this mystery will be solved  in a potential season 2.  [Applause]  foreign  [Music]
 

